           THE THREE ACQUISITION STAGE VALUES ON THIS PAGE MOVED:
             LOI__c.Submit_for_Approval   Prepare/Review -> Under Review   🔴 NOW SHARED
             LOI__c.Mark_Countered        Sent           -> Submitted      still acquisition-only
             LOI__c.Mark_Completed        Sent           -> Submitted      still acquisition-only

           'Submitted', 'Negotiation' and 'Signed' are on Acquisition_LOI ONLY, so the two Mark_*
           entries remain self-limiting by exactly the retracted mechanism - it still holds for
           them, it just no longer holds UNIVERSALLY. The four disposition-only values
           ('Received', 'Countered by DPEG', 'Counter Received from Buyer', 'Executed') are
           likewise untouched, so BOTH disposition Mark_* entries are unaffected.

           THE TWO Advance_Stage ENTRIES ARE UNCHANGED HERE, but the field behind them is not:
           Is_Advance_Allowed__c now carries a RecordType.DeveloperName test inside its approval
           clause, because 'Under Review' being shared would otherwise have hidden the
           DISPOSITION Advance Stage button at step 2 of 5. That is a real sell-side outage
           averted in the formula, not on this page; see that field's header.

         🔴 OPEN RESIDUAL - Submit for Approval IS NO LONGER SELF-LIMITING, AND IT IS NOT CLOSED
         HERE. 'Under Review' is carried by BOTH record types, so an acquisition driver opening a
         DISPOSITION LOI at 'Under Review' will now see Submit for Approval. Clicking it targets
         Opportunity.LOI_Approval, and a disposition LOI has no Opportunity__c, so it fails
         LOUDLY rather than doing something wrong.

         WHY IT WAS NOT CLOSED, STATED SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT ASSUME IT WAS MISSED:
           - A FLEXIPAGE VISIBILITY RULE CANNOT EXPRESS A RECORD-TYPE TEST DIRECTLY. That is
             asserted in NDA__c.Is_Decline_Allowed__c's header ("so the discriminator has to be a
             field on the record") and no flexipage in this repo carries a {!Record.RecordType.*}
             criterion - a sweep of every flexipage found zero. ⚠ REPO-ASSERTED AND UNMEASURED:
             nobody has tried it in this org and been refused. Falsifying it is the cheapest of
             the three remedies below.
           - The in-repo remedy is a formula-checkbox discriminator (the Is_Decline_Allowed__c
             shape). A NEW field arrives with NO field permissions for ANY profile, and a
             visibility rule referencing a field the running user cannot read evaluates FALSE -
             so adding one here would make THREE currently-working acquisition buttons newly
             dependent on a brand-new field's FLS across every acquisition permission set plus
             any profile-provisioned persona this repo cannot see. That is precisely the trade
             Is_Decline_Allowed__c's own header refuses to make for the acquisition Advance Stage
             rule.
           - The residual is the SAME CLASS this page already names and accepts one paragraph
             above, for Advance Stage, and it fails loudly.

         THREE REMEDIES, IF THE RESIDUAL IS JUDGED UNACCEPTABLE. Take the decision explicitly;
         do not let it arrive as a side effect of some other change:
           (a) TRY {!Record.RecordType.DeveloperName} EQUAL Acquisition_LOI as a third criterion
               and see whether the deploy accepts it AND the renderer honours it. Cheapest, and
               it falsifies the repo assertion above either way. A deploy that succeeds is NOT
               sufficient evidence - this page has MEASURED a construct that deploys, survives a
               retrieve and is then ignored by the renderer. Probe in BOTH directions.
           (b) ADD Is_Acquisition_LOI__c, a formula checkbox = RecordType.DeveloperName
               = "Acquisition_LOI", and grant it read FLS in the SAME change to every set that
               grants Is_Advance_Allowed__c. Proven shape, known cost.
           (c) ADD {!Record.Is_Advance_Allowed__c} EQUAL false as a third criterion. It happens to
               discriminate correctly here - on a disposition LOI at 'Under Review' that formula
               is TRUE, on an unapproved acquisition LOI at 'Under Review' it is FALSE - and it
               costs no new field and no new FLS. ⚠ NOT RECOMMENDED: it couples Submit for
               Approval's visibility to a formula written for Advance Stage, so a future edit to
               that formula would break this button silently. Recorded because it is genuinely
               the cheapest and someone will find it.

         ⚠ TIMING - THIS FILE MUST DEPLOY AT P3-D2 WITH THE APEX, NOT EARLIER, AND THE REASON IS
         NOT SYMMETRY. The three repointed criteria are correct only AFTER the row migration; a
         transitional duplicate-entry shape (one entry per value, old and new, the shape the two
         Advance_Stage entries already prove works in this org) was considered and REJECTED,
         because it would make Counter and Completed visible at 'Submitted' while
         c/loiMarkCountered still passes the hardcoded constant 'Counter'. A driver clicking it
         would then write a RETIRED value onto a migrated LOI - moving the record backwards into
         a stage the new sequence has no entry for. A hidden button for the length of one
         maintenance window is strictly better than a visible button wired to the wrong constant.
